1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,000
♪ ♪ ♪

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:07,380
Hi, I'm Dessi Zaharieva and I'm here to talk to you

3
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,920
about managing activities of daily living and diabetes.

4
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:14,640
A little about me, I'm a Certified Exercise

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:18,330
Physiologist, a second degree black belt in TaeKwonDo,

6
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:21,600
a purple belt in Brazilian Jiujitsu, and I've

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,360
lived with type one diabetes for over 28 years.

8
00:00:25,169 --> 00:00:27,270
In that time, I've learned a lot about

9
00:00:27,270 --> 00:00:29,610
managing diabetes and staying active,

10
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:32,970
both for myself and for the patients I work with.

11
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:37,500
Today, I wanna talk about something we don't often discuss as part of

12
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:42,245
standardized diabetes education, activities of daily living, or ADLs.

13
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:48,150
When we think about staying active and managing blood glucose levels,

14
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:50,790
we usually picture structured workouts like

15
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:52,845
running, swimming, or going to the gym.

16
00:00:53,985 --> 00:00:56,565
But ADLs are the everyday tasks we do

17
00:00:56,625 --> 00:00:59,114
like cleaning, shopping, or yard work.

18
00:00:59,955 --> 00:01:03,614
These might not seem much like activities per se, but they

19
00:01:03,614 --> 00:01:06,405
can still have an impact on your blood glucose levels,

20
00:01:06,765 --> 00:01:09,675
especially if they involve a lot of whole body movement.

21
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
♪ ♪ ♪

22
00:01:16,185 --> 00:01:17,745
So what are ADLs?

23
00:01:18,285 --> 00:01:21,105
ADLs are things you do every day or more

24
00:01:21,105 --> 00:01:23,685
often to take care of yourself and your home.

25
00:01:24,345 --> 00:01:27,375
They typically fall into two general categories.

26
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:30,855
Basic ADLs: these are things like showering,

27
00:01:30,914 --> 00:01:32,835
brushing your teeth, or getting dressed.

28
00:01:33,164 --> 00:01:35,175
They're usually not very intense, so they

29
00:01:35,175 --> 00:01:37,035
may not affect blood glucose levels much.

30
00:01:38,055 --> 00:01:41,895
And instrumental ADLs: are tasks like vacuuming,

31
00:01:41,955 --> 00:01:45,315
gardening, walking around the store, or doing yard work.

32
00:01:46,005 --> 00:01:48,705
These activities often use larger muscle groups

33
00:01:48,705 --> 00:01:50,805
and can make your blood glucose levels drop,

34
00:01:51,105 --> 00:01:52,935
especially if they take a long time.

35
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
♪ ♪ ♪

36
00:02:00,855 --> 00:02:04,005
Some ADLs like cleaning or gardening work

37
00:02:04,005 --> 00:02:06,225
your muscles and make your heart beat faster.

38
00:02:06,810 --> 00:02:09,810
They're not as intense as a structured workout, but they

39
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:12,510
may still lower your blood glucose levels over time.

40
00:02:13,109 --> 00:02:16,260
For example, ADLs get your muscles moving.

41
00:02:16,859 --> 00:02:18,540
They make your heart beat faster.

42
00:02:19,019 --> 00:02:21,299
They might cause large drops in glucose

43
00:02:21,299 --> 00:02:23,835
levels, particularly if they last a long time.

44
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,640
ADLs can be a bit sneaky to manage because often you're

45
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,070
focused on the last minute holiday shopping you need

46
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:35,220
to do or running to meet your child spur of the moment.

47
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:37,530
Then all of a sudden you might be going low.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
♪ ♪ ♪

49
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:47,670
Have you ever experienced what we call the mall low after hours of walking

50
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:52,154
around a mall or a big store like Costco, you feel the low blood sugars?

51
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:55,815
That's because walking for a long time can lower glucose

52
00:02:55,815 --> 00:02:58,950
levels even if it doesn't feel like structured exercise.

53
00:03:00,195 --> 00:03:02,115
A lot of the time when we go to pick up

54
00:03:02,115 --> 00:03:05,025
groceries, it's not exactly a planned activity,

55
00:03:05,355 --> 00:03:07,995
so it's easy to forget a quick snack or to

56
00:03:07,995 --> 00:03:10,635
set an activity preset well before you go.

57
00:03:11,355 --> 00:03:12,675
So how do you manage this?

58
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
♪ ♪ ♪

59
00:03:16,455 --> 00:03:20,895
Let's talk about some tips for managing blood glucose levels during ADLs.

60
00:03:21,705 --> 00:03:23,535
One, watch your blood glucose levels.

61
00:03:24,644 --> 00:03:28,605
Check your continuous glucose monitoring or CGM system often.

62
00:03:28,905 --> 00:03:33,124
For example, at least once every 20 to 30 minutes during the ADL.

63
00:03:34,065 --> 00:03:36,704
Two, eat fast acting carbs.

64
00:03:37,214 --> 00:03:40,635
Carry snacks like glucose tablets or candy and have

65
00:03:40,635 --> 00:03:43,005
some If your blood glucose levels start to drop.

66
00:03:43,755 --> 00:03:47,654
Consider eating fast acting carbs before and during the ADL

67
00:03:47,804 --> 00:03:50,685
if you often experience drops in glucose with that activity.

68
00:03:51,930 --> 00:03:54,330
Three, use activity presets.

69
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:57,900
Consider setting the Tidepool Loop walking preset,

69
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:02,070
or a custom preset with a lower amount of overall insulin if

70
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:04,890
you know you'll be doing an ADL for a long period of time.

71
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
♪ ♪ ♪

72
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:15,570
As we get older, we might spend more time on ADLs and

73
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:19,230
less time doing intense exercise, and that's okay.

74
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:21,990
It's still important to notice how these everyday

75
00:04:21,990 --> 00:04:24,210
tasks could affect your blood glucose levels.

76
00:04:25,245 --> 00:04:27,570
Try to notice any changes in the type and

77
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:30,450
intensity of activity you do as you get older.

78
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
♪ ♪ ♪

79
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:38,490
Now let's talk about the relationship between intensity and activity.

80
00:04:39,490 --> 00:04:42,810
Tidepool Loop includes an RPE scale, which is a

81
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:45,750
rating of perceived exertion or intensity scale,

82
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:49,469
and this helps you track how an activity's intensity

83
00:04:49,469 --> 00:04:52,409
feels and how it might affect your blood glucose levels.

84
00:04:53,265 --> 00:04:57,375
For example, an exercise might feel hard the first time you do it,

85
00:04:57,735 --> 00:05:01,455
but as you become more comfortable with that activity or you increase your

86
00:05:01,455 --> 00:05:06,135
fitness levels, the intensity of that activity may be lower than before.

87
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
♪ ♪ ♪

88
00:05:10,575 --> 00:05:14,025
Now I'm going to wrap up with a self-reflection exercise.

89
00:05:14,985 --> 00:05:19,335
Over the next week, take 15 minutes to think about how ADLs

90
00:05:19,335 --> 00:05:22,305
affect your blood glucose levels in different settings.

91
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,810
A few things you can ask yourself:

92
00:05:25,710 --> 00:05:28,500
what chores or tasks do I do every day?

93
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:32,550
Do any of these activities make my blood glucose drop?

94
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,800
Are there patterns in when or how these tasks affect you?

95
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,730
How long does the activity last and how intense

96
00:05:41,730 --> 00:05:44,310
is it when my blood glucose changes the most?

97
00:05:45,570 --> 00:05:48,150
How does my career impact the work that I do?

98
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:52,140
If you work remotely or in person or both,

99
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:55,409
consider how this impacts your diabetes management.

100
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:01,020
Understanding how daily tasks impact your diabetes can help you

101
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:05,070
plan ahead and make it easier to handle these everyday challenges.

102
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:15,000
♪ ♪ ♪
